<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vila Velha</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vila Velha is a city in the Brazilian state of Espírito Santo, the oldest in the state, founded in 1535 by the donatary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-fernandes-coutinho">Vasco Fernandes Coutinho</a>. Indigenous attacks led the settlers to move the capital to the nearby island of Vitória in 1551, and the first town became the 'old town'. The hilltop Convent of Penha, founded in the 16th century, overlooks it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vila-velha.jpg" alt="Vila Velha" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HVL · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vila_Velha_ES_a_partir_do_Convento_da_Penha.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Vila Velha, whose name means Old Village, lies on the Atlantic coast of the state of Espírito Santo in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, about 10 km south of the state capital, Vitória, with which it forms part of the Greater Vitória Metropolitan Area.</p><p>The terrain is mostly flat, the municipal seat standing about 4 m above sea level, and the 32 km of coastline is almost entirely lined with beaches. Several hills rise from the plain, among them Morro da Penha at 154 m, which carries the Penha Convent, and Morro do Moreno at 164 m, which preserves a remnant of the Atlantic Forest, the original vegetation of the area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 16th century the area was contested by three Indigenous groups: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goitaca">Goitacá</a> from the south, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aimore">Aimoré</a> from the interior and the Tupiniquim from the north. On 23 May 1535 the Portuguese Vasco Fernandes Coutinho, grantee of the Captaincy of Espírito Santo, landed at the present-day Prainha, which the Indigenous people called Piratininga, from the caravel Glória with 60 men. He founded the Vila do Espírito Santo, now Vila Velha, so named because it was the Sunday of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pentecost">Pentecost</a>. The settlement became the captaincy's capital, and the seat was later transferred to Vitória.</p><p>Agriculture sustained the town for much of its history and declined in the 20th century, giving way to commerce, tourism and industry. The chocolate factory Garoto was founded there in August 1929. The port terminal is one of the largest in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. Theatre has been part of the town since the colonial period: plays written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> were performed in the late 16th century, among them Na Visitação de Santa Izabel of 1597 by Father Joseph of Anchieta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Vila Velha is the oldest city in the state and holds several historic landmarks, including the Church of Our Lady of the Rosary, the Fort of São Francisco Xavier de Piratininga and the Santa Luzia Lighthouse. The Penha Convent, one of the oldest churches in Espírito Santo, was begun around 1558 under Friar Pedro Palácios and was designated a national cultural heritage site by the heritage institute Iphan in 1943.</p><p>Its high altar, built around 1800 in the Rococo style, has over 200 pieces of 19 types of marble. The city has about half a million inhabitants, and its anniversary on 23 May is also a state holiday.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vila Velha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1534 King John III granted the captaincy of Espírito Santo to Vasco Fernandes Coutinho, who landed in 1535 and founded <b>Vila Velha</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-espirito-santo">Captaincy of Espírito Santo</a></li></ul></section>
